Big O Notation is a mathematical notation that describes the behavior of a
value (most commonly denoted as <Math math={n} />) as it reaches infinity. While the
mathematical explanation is standard, there's a nuance in programming: it
applies to both **Time Complexity** and **Space Complexity**.

## Dictionary

* **Time Complexity**: Resources needed by the CPU to finish evaluating the algorithm at runtime.
* **Space Complexity**: Memory resources needed to complete the algorithm.
* **Compile Time**: Not measured with Big O. This is the time your compiler or interpreter takes to translate code.
* **Runtime**: When the program is actually executed. This is what LeetCode measures.

---

## Analysis



### <Math math={o1} /> - Constant Complexity

Common for:
* Pushing an item to the front of a vector.
* Looking up an item in a Hash Map/Set.
* Getting the first element of a linked list.

### <Math math={logN} /> - Logarithmic Complexity

Primary use is **Binary Search**. It is the gold standard for searching sorted data sets.

### <Math math={oN} /> - Linear Complexity

Doing at least one full iteration on your data.  
> **Note:** Removing the last item of a vector is <Math math={o1} />, but removing the
> first item is <Math math={oN} /> because you must rearrange all subsequent items.

### <Math math={nLogN} /> - Linearithmic Complexity

Usually where efficient sorting algorithms fall: **Quicksort**, **Heapsort**, and **Merge sort**.

### <Math math={nSquared} /> - Quadratic Complexity

Commonly seen in nested loops or matrix traversals (`arr[i][j]`). Bubble Sort also falls here.

