## Optimizing for Memory vs. Space

Finding prime numbers is a great way to visualize this trade-off.

<YouTube id="fwxjMKBMR7s" title="Finding Primes - b001" />

### 1. Trial Division

The most memory-efficient but slowest method. It relies on the fact
that divisors are mirrored around the square root of <Math math={n} />.

* **Space**: <Math math={o1} />
* **Time**: <Math math={sqrtN} />

### 2. Sieve of Eratosthenes

The quickest for bulk evaluation.

* **Time**: <Math math={sieveTime} />
* **Space**: <Math math={oN} /> (requires a boolean array up to `n`)

### 3. Dijkstra's Approach

A balanced approach using a "pool" of tuples to track multiples, avoiding the
massive memory hit of the Sieve while staying faster than Trial Division.

---

## Not only Big O

Big O describes the **worst-case scenario**. Mathematically:

<Math math={bigODef} />

### Big Omega (<Math math={omega} />)

Describes the **best-case scenario** (lower bound):

<Math math={bigOmegaDef} />

### Big Theta (<Math math={theta} />)

Describes the **tightest bound** (average case):

<Math math={bigThetaDef} />

---

## Summary

* **Big O** is like <Math math={leq} /> (Worst case / Upper bound)
* **Big {omega}** is like <Math math={geq} /> (Best case / Lower bound)
* **Big {theta}** is like `==` (Tightly bounded / Average case)

Determining what to optimize depends on your specific use case. In game dev,
memory efficiency is often a speed improvement. In web apps, raw CPU runtime
might be your bottleneck.

